The TLS_CA_Signed directory is already populated with test certificates built from:
	-generate_certs.sh
	-generate_java_keystore.sh
================================================================================
The client directory has all you need to test:
	-for c processes
		-jal_subscriber_v1_client.cert.pem (client public key certificate)
		-jal_subscriber_v1_client.key.pem (client private key)
		-trust_store_dir
			-jal_publisher_v1_server.cert.pem and hash name link (ex:33e17597.0)
			-jalop_ca.cert.pem and hash name link (ex:281b40c4.0) (CA cert)
	-for java processes
		-jal_subscriber_v1_client.jks (client private key and public key certificate)
		-jal_subscriber_v1_client-remotes.jks (trusted certificates: server and CA)
================================================================================
The server directory has all you need to test:
	-for c processes
		-jal_publisher_v1_server.cert.pem (server public key certificate)
		-jal_publisher_v1_server.key.pem (server private key)
		-trust_store_dir
			-jal_subscriber_v1_client.cert.pem and hash name link (ex:e6fbbcf6.0)
			-jalop_ca.cert.pem and hash name link (ex:281b40c4.0) (CA cert)
	-for java processes
		-jal_publisher_v1_server.jks (server private key and public key)
		-jal_publisher_v1_server-remotes.jks (trusted certificates: client and CA)
================================================================================
The public certificates for server and client are built with alt_names:
	[ alt_name ]
	DNS.0 = localhost
	DNS.1 = jal_subscriber_v1_client
	IP.0 = 127.0.0.1

	[ alt_name ]
	DNS.0 = localhost
	DNS.1 = jal_publisher_v1_server
	IP.0 = 127.0.0.1

	So when running publisher and subscriber on different machines you can add
	entries to their /etc/hosts file.
	example:
		192.168.1.100 jal_subscriber_v1_client (on server)
		192.168.1.101 jal_publisher_v1_server (on client)
================================================================================
If you want to build your own set of certificates you can alter these files:
	-ca.cnf (the DN variables)
	-client.cnf (the DN variables and alt names)
	-server.cnf (the DN variables and alt names)
	-generate_certs.sh (the 5 variables at top)
	-generate_java_keystore.sh (the 4 variables at top)
	-then run ./generate_certs.sh which will call generate_java_keystore.sh at end
================================================================================
You can have your in house certificates too. Refer to the client directory
and server directory sections above for the correct artifacts needed or look
below for configuration examples.
================================================================================
------------------------ jal_subscribe.cfg -------------------------------------
# The path to the private key, used for TLS.
private_key = "<path>/jal_subscriber_v1_client.key.pem";

# The path to the public cert, used for TLS.
public_cert = "<path>/TLS/client/jal_subscriber_v1_client.cert.pem";

# Directory containing the certificates for the remote peers.
remote_cert = "<path>/TLS/client/trust_store_dir";
================================================================================
---------------------------- jald.cfg ------------------------------------------
# The path to the private key, used for TLS.
private_key = "<path>/jal_publisher_v1_server.key.pem";

# The path to the public cert, used for TLS.
public_cert = "<path>/jal_publisher_v1_server.cert.pem";

# Directory file containing the certificates for the remote peers.
remote_cert_dir = "<path>/trust_store_dir";
================================================================================
----------------- sampleSubscriber.json (snippet)---------------------------
  "ssl": {
    "Key Store Passphrase": "changeit",
    "Key Store": "<jjnl root dir>/jnl_test/certs/TLS_CA_Signed/client/jal_subscriber_v1_client.jks",

    "Trust Store Passphrase": "changeit",
    "Trust Store": "<jjnl root dir>/jnl_test/certs/TLS_CA_Signed/client/jal_subscriber_v1_client-remotes.jks",
  }
================================================================================
